WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.751
APIs should control what they return as carefully as what they accept.

2
00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:17.071
A C# application may validate incoming data well and still expose too much through response objects, error payloads, exported files, debug endpoints, or generated documentation.

3
00:00:17.071 --> 00:00:19.970
Output design is a sensitive data control.

4
00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:31.243
DTOs and response models help prevent accidental exposure of internal fields, flags, tokens, secrets, audit notes, administrative data, or unrelated tenant data.

5
00:00:31.243 --> 00:00:39.939
A response contract should represent the specific client need, not everything available on a domain object or database entity.

6
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:44.126
Model binding and deserialization produce untrusted input.

7
00:00:44.126 --> 00:00:48.313
Bound data still needs validation and authorization checks.

8
00:00:48.313 --> 00:01:00.472
A client-supplied user ID, tenant ID, role flag, price, status, or internal reference should be verified against trusted server-side data before it affects state or visibility.

9
00:01:00.472 --> 00:01:08.283
Over-posting and over-sharing can occur when domain models are reused directly as request or response contracts.

10
00:01:08.283 --> 00:01:22.696
JSON serialization settings, debug endpoints, OpenAPI documentation, error payloads, exported files, and pagination metadata can reveal more than intended if they are generated from broad internal objects.

11
00:01:22.696 --> 00:01:32.601
Browser storage, hidden fields, query strings, mobile app storage, and client-side logs should not hold secrets or authoritative permissions.

12
00:01:32.601 --> 00:01:42.908
A client can help display data and collect input, but server-side policy must decide what data is allowed to leave and what submitted values are trusted.
